update posts set content = '<p>The first question is always the same, and it is not about the boat. It is how many people are coming. Answer that and the rest of the decision makes itself, because on the Gold Coast Broadwater there are really only two shapes of end-of-year party on the water, and the number of guests picks one.</p><h2>If you have 20 to 60 people: book seats, not a boat</h2><p>Most work parties are smaller than the room they get offered. A group of 30 does not need a 150-guest vessel and should not be paying for one.</p><p>The Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise takes 100 guests a sailing and sells seat by seat. It runs every Saturday and Sunday through December, departing 5:00 PM from Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon at Main Beach and back at 7:30 PM. A group of 30 books 30 seats and shares the boat with other guests, which for a casual team drinks is usually the point rather than the compromise.</p><p><strong>The December sailings:</strong> Saturday 5th, Sunday 6th, Saturday 12th, Sunday 13th, Saturday 19th, Sunday 20th, Saturday 26th and Sunday 27th. There is no sailing on 24, 25 or 31 December.</p><p><strong>What a seat costs:</strong> $79 an adult, $149 for two adults together, $55 for a child aged 3 to 13, $11 for an infant under 3.</p><h2>If you have 60 to 150 people, or you want the boat to yourselves: charter</h2><p>A whole-vessel charter is a different product. You set the date, the time, the route, the catering and the bar, and nobody else is aboard.</p><p><strong>Sun Goddess</strong> is 114 feet, takes up to 135 guests, and has two entertainment decks, two licensed bars and a dual-level galley. <strong>Mermaid Spirit</strong> is a 100 foot tri-deck catamaran for up to 150 guests by day, with a chef''s kitchen, a stinger pool and a sun lounge, and 11 en suite cabins that sleep 22 if the party turns into a weekend.</p><p>Charter is quoted rather than ticketed, because the price moves with the date, the hours, the catering and the bar package. There is no list rate, and any hourly figure for these vessels published on a broker''s website is not ours.</p><h2>The three things that decide a December date</h2><p><strong>Saturdays go first.</strong> Eight sunset sailings exist in December and only four of them are Saturdays. For a charter, December Saturdays are the scarcest thing on the calendar and they are gone earliest.</p><p><strong>Catering is a lead time, not a menu.</strong> Grazing boards and canapes are quick. Private chef fine dining for 100 is not. The menu conversation should happen at the same time as the date conversation, not after it.</p><p><strong>Transport is the detail people forget.</strong> Maxi taxis are the usual answer for a group, and the pontoon has free parking. Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon is about ten minutes from Surfers Paradise and fifteen from Broadbeach.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How many people can you fit on a Gold Coast party boat?</h3><p>Sun Goddess carries up to 135 guests on a day charter and Mermaid Spirit up to 150, so a whole-vessel charter suits groups from about 20 up to 150. For a group of 20 to 60, the Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise has 100 seats a sailing and can be booked seat by seat instead.</p><h3>How much is a Christmas party boat on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>Seats on the Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise are $79 an adult, or $149 for two adults together. A whole-vessel charter is quoted on the date, the hours, the catering and the bar package, so it needs an enquiry rather than a list price.</p><h3>What dates are available for a December boat party on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>The sunset cruise sails every Saturday and Sunday in December: the 5th, 6th, 12th, 13th, 19th, 20th, 26th and 27th. There is no sailing on 24, 25 or 31 December. Charter dates are separate and are confirmed on enquiry.</p><h3>Where does the boat leave from?</h3><p>Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon, Main Beach, Gold Coast. Roughly ten minutes from Surfers Paradise and fifteen from Broadbeach, with free parking at the pontoon.</p><h3>Can we bring our own food or drinks?</h3><p>No. Both vessels have two bars each, with bar tab or consumption options, and catering is arranged through our culinary partners. No external food or beverage is permitted on board.</p><h2>Where to start</h2><p>If you know your numbers and your date, the enquiry form is the fastest route: it goes straight to the team who hold the calendar. If you are between the two options, say how many people and roughly when, and we will tell you which one makes sense rather than which one is bigger.</p>', updated_at = now()
where slug = 'christmas-end-of-year-boat-parties-gold-coast'
  and content like '%## %'
  and content not like '%<h2>%';

update posts set content = '<p>Most people booking a sunset cruise have not been on one, and the listings tend to sell the view rather than explain the evening. Here is what the two and a half hours are actually made of.</p><h2>The shape of the evening</h2><p>Boarding opens at 4:30 PM at Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon, Main Beach. Departure is 5:00 PM. You are back at the pontoon at 7:30 PM, so 150 minutes on the water.</p><p>The route runs north through the Broadwater toward the islands, turns, and comes back with the sun going down over Surfers Paradise. That direction matters: the skyline is lit from behind on the way out and in front of you on the way home, which is when most people take the photograph they keep.</p><p>The vessel is Sun Goddess, 114 feet, with two entertainment decks and two licensed bars. There are 100 seats on a sailing, so it is a shared cruise rather than a private charter, and there is room to move between the indoor lounge, the open foredeck and the upper sun deck as the light changes.</p><h2>What is included and what is extra</h2><p>Included: the cruise, light commentary on the surrounds, drinks service, and a nibbles share plate for two.</p><p>Available on board as extras: cocktails, snacks, a sparkling or beer on arrival, and an on-board photo opportunity.</p><p>No external food or beverage is permitted on board, so the bar and the kitchen are the only sources once you are aboard.</p><h2>What to wear, and the one thing people get wrong</h2><p>Smart casual. Flat shoes are better than heels on a deck. The thing people get wrong is the temperature: the Broadwater in the evening is noticeably cooler than the car park, especially once the sun is down and the boat is moving, so bring a layer even in summer. Sunglasses are more useful on the outbound leg than you would expect, because you are heading into the glare.</p><h2>What you might see</h2><p>Marine life is often about in the Seaway as the yacht navigates it: dolphins, turtles and dugongs are all regular. None of that is guaranteed on a given evening, and anyone promising you a dolphin on a sunset cruise is selling you something they cannot deliver.</p><p>What is reliable is the light. The Broadwater is a sheltered body of water with the skyline on one side and the Spit on the other, which is why the evening works whichever way the weather goes.</p><h2>When it sails, and what it costs</h2><p>Saturdays and Sundays, departing 5:00 PM, with one extra sailing on Friday 20 November. There is no sailing on 24, 25 or 31 December or on 1 January.</p><p>Adult $79. Two adults together $149. Child aged 3 to 13 $55. Infant under 3 $11.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What time does the Gold Coast sunset cruise depart?</h3><p>Boarding opens at 4:30 PM, Sun Goddess departs at 5:00 PM and returns at 7:30 PM, from Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon at Main Beach.</p><h3>How long is the sunset cruise?</h3><p>150 minutes on the water, from a 5:00 PM departure to a 7:30 PM return.</p><h3>What happens on a sunset cruise?</h3><p>You board at 4:30 PM, depart at 5:00, run north through the Broadwater toward the islands, then turn and come back with the sun setting over Surfers Paradise. Drinks service and a nibbles share plate for two are included, with cocktails, snacks and arrival drinks available on board.</p><h3>What should I wear on a Gold Coast sunset cruise?</h3><p>Smart casual, flat shoes rather than heels, and a layer for the trip home. The Broadwater is cooler in the evening than the shore, particularly once the boat is moving.</p><h3>Is the sunset cruise a private charter?</h3><p>No. It is a shared cruise with 100 seats a sailing, booked seat by seat. Sun Goddess is also available as a private charter for up to 135 guests, which is quoted separately.</p><h3>Can you see wildlife on the sunset cruise?</h3><p>Dolphins, turtles and dugongs are often about as the yacht navigates the Seaway. Sightings are not guaranteed on any particular sailing.</p><h2>Booking</h2><p>Seats are on the ticket page and confirm instantly. If you are a group of 20 or more, say so when you book, because the seating works better when the group is seated together rather than scattered across three decks.</p>', updated_at = now()
where slug = 'what-happens-on-a-gold-coast-sunset-cruise'
  and content like '%## %'
  and content not like '%<h2>%';

update posts set content = '<p>A wedding on the water is not a venue with a different view. It is a venue with a fixed guest list, a fixed timeline and a departure time, and those three things decide most of what follows. Here is the order the decisions actually come in, rather than the order the inspiration photos suggest.</p><h2>1. Your guest count picks the vessel, not the other way round</h2><p>This is first because nothing else can be settled until it is. There are two vessels and the number decides which one.</p><p><strong>Sun Goddess</strong> takes up to 135 guests on a day charter. It is 114 feet, with two entertainment decks, two licensed bars and a dual-level galley, and the foredeck is the part most couples are picturing.</p><p><strong>Mermaid Spirit</strong> takes up to 150 guests by day. It is a 100 foot tri-deck catamaran with a chef''s kitchen, and it has 11 en suite cabins that sleep 22, so it is the one to ask about if the wedding party is staying aboard.</p><p>A boat has a hard ceiling in a way a function room does not. A room can squeeze in ten more; a vessel cannot. So the guest list needs to be close to final earlier than it would be on land, and it is worth deciding before you fall in love with a particular deck.</p><h2>2. The ceremony can happen on board, and the celebrant is yours to book</h2><p>Yes, you can marry on the foredeck with the Broadwater behind you. What people do not always realise is that the celebrant is a separate booking: you need a registered marriage celebrant, and they come to the boat. We can recommend partners if you do not already have someone.</p><p>Treat it as its own line on the plan with its own confirmation, because it is the one element that cannot be improvised on the day.</p><h2>3. The light decides your ceremony time</h2><p>On land you pick a time and the light is whatever it is. On the Broadwater the light is the reason you are there, so run it the other way: decide when you want the ceremony photographed, then build the timeline backwards from it.</p><p>A late afternoon ceremony puts the vows in soft light and the reception into golden hour and dusk, which is the sequence most couples are after. An earlier start gives you brighter, higher-contrast photographs and a longer reception before dark. Neither is better. They are different evenings, and choosing deliberately is better than discovering which one you booked.</p><h2>4. Catering is a lead time before it is a menu</h2><p>Everything from canapes and grazing boards through buffets to multi-course seated dinners and private chef fine dining is available, through our culinary partners. The thing to know early is that the ambition level sets the lead time. Canapes for 60 is a short conversation. A seated multi-course dinner for 130 is not, and it needs to be settled at roughly the same time as the date rather than two months out.</p><p>Bar service runs on a bar tab or by consumption, and drinks packages are available. No external food or beverage comes aboard, so the catering conversation is the whole food conversation.</p><h2>5. Styling, flowers and the things you can add</h2><p>Floral arrangements, table styling and custom decor can all be coordinated through the event team, so you are not obliged to bring a separate supplier for each. Entertainment is available as a DJ or a band. Photography and videography can be arranged, and on some charters watersports are available if the day turns into an afternoon rather than an evening.</p><p>The useful question to ask is not what is possible but what is coordinated: the more that runs through one team, the fewer vendors you are managing from a boat that has already left the pontoon.</p><h2>6. Have the wet weather answer before you need it</h2><p>Both vessels have covered deck areas and interior spaces, so an event runs whatever the sky does, and there is always a contingency plan. Ask for yours in writing when you book rather than on the morning. It usually comes down to where the ceremony moves to, and knowing that in advance is the difference between a change of plan and a crisis.</p><h2>7. Getting everyone there</h2><p>Departure is from Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon at Main Beach, roughly ten minutes from Surfers Paradise and fifteen from Broadbeach, with free parking at the pontoon. For a guest list in the dozens, maxi taxis are the usual answer.</p><p>The one instruction worth repeating on the invitation: a boat leaves. Put a be-there-by time on the invite that is well before the departure time, because the vessel cannot wait for the car that took a wrong turn.</p><h2>What it costs</h2><p>Charter is quoted rather than priced from a list, because the figure moves with the date, the hours, the guest count, the catering and the bar. There is no list rate, and any hourly figure for these vessels published on a broker''s website is not ours.</p><p>If you want a real number, the fastest route is to send a date, a rough guest count and the kind of catering you have in mind. That is enough to quote against.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How many guests can you have at a yacht wedding on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>Sun Goddess accommodates up to 135 guests for a day charter and Mermaid Spirit up to 150. Mermaid Spirit also has 11 en suite cabins sleeping 22, for a wedding party staying on board.</p><h3>Can you legally get married on a boat on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>Yes. The ceremony can be held on the foredeck with the Broadwater as the backdrop. You need to book a registered marriage celebrant separately, and they join the vessel. We can recommend partners if you do not have one.</p><h3>Where does a Gold Coast yacht wedding depart from?</h3><p>Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon, Main Beach, about ten minutes from Surfers Paradise and fifteen from Broadbeach. There is free parking at the pontoon.</p><h3>What happens if it rains on a yacht wedding?</h3><p>Both vessels have covered deck areas and interior spaces, so the event runs regardless of the weather, and a contingency plan is agreed in advance. Ask for yours in writing at the time of booking.</p><h3>How much does a yacht wedding cost on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>It is quoted rather than listed, because the price moves with the date, the hours, the guest count, the catering and the bar package. A date and a rough guest count are enough to quote against. Any hourly rate for these vessels published on a broker''s site is not the operator''s.</p><h3>Can you arrange flowers, styling and entertainment?</h3><p>Yes. Floral arrangements, table styling and custom decor are coordinated through the event team, and a DJ or band, photography and videography can all be arranged.</p><h2>Where to start</h2><p>Send a date, a guest count and the catering you are imagining. The date is the scarce part, particularly on a Saturday, so it is worth asking about before the rest is decided.</p>', updated_at = now()
where slug = 'gold-coast-yacht-wedding-planning-guide'
  and content like '%## %'
  and content not like '%<h2>%';

update posts set content = '<p>This is the most asked question in the category and the least honestly answered. Most pages that rank for it give you a range wide enough to be useless, or an hourly rate that turns out not to be the operator''s. So here is the mechanism instead: what moves the number, and what a real quote needs from you.</p><h2>Why there is no list price, and why that is not evasion</h2><p>A charter is not a ticket. A ticketed cruise has one price because it has one route, one duration and a fixed inclusion list. A charter has none of those fixed until you set them, and each one moves the cost:</p><ul><li><p><strong>The date.</strong> A Saturday in December and a Tuesday in February are not the same product, because demand for the first is finite and the second is not.</p></li><li><p><strong>The hours.</strong> Crew, fuel and catering all scale with duration, and a three hour charter and a six hour charter are not proportional, because the fixed costs of getting underway are the same for both.</p></li><li><p><strong>The guest count.</strong> This drives catering, bar and crew, and it determines which vessel you need.</p></li><li><p><strong>The catering and bar.</strong> Canapes for 40 and a seated multi-course dinner for 130 are different businesses. The bar can run as a tab or by consumption, which also changes how the final figure lands.</p></li><li><p><strong>The extras.</strong> Entertainment, styling, photography, watersports. Each is optional and each is additive.</p></li></ul><p>A list price that covered all of that would have to be a worst-case number, and you would be quoted something higher than what you actually want.</p><h2>The hourly rates you will find online are usually not the operator''s</h2><p>If you search for a specific vessel you will often find an hourly rate on a broker or boat-hire aggregator. Those figures are set by the site publishing them, not by the operator, and they frequently include that site''s own margin or describe a package the operator does not sell.</p><p>A worked example from this market: a boat hire aggregator currently publishes an hourly figure for Sun Goddess. Boat Time does not publish an hourly rate for Sun Goddess at all. Whichever number is right, only one of those two parties takes the booking.</p><p>The practical advice is narrow: treat any price you did not get from the operator as an indication of what a middleman wants to charge, and check it against a direct quote before you budget around it.</p><h2>What a quote needs from you, and why it is only four things</h2><ol><li><p><strong>A date,</strong> or two or three candidate dates.</p></li><li><p><strong>A rough guest count.</strong> Rough is fine. The difference between 40 and 120 matters; the difference between 48 and 52 does not.</p></li><li><p><strong>How long you want to be out.</strong> If you do not know, say what the occasion is and a sensible duration gets suggested.</p></li><li><p><strong>What kind of food and drink you are imagining.</strong> Canapes, a BBQ, a buffet, or a seated dinner. That is enough.</p></li></ol><p>With those four, a quote is a short conversation. Without them, any number anyone gives you is a guess dressed as an answer.</p><h2>Where a ticketed cruise is the better answer</h2><p>Sometimes the honest answer to "what does a charter cost" is that you do not want a charter.</p><p>If you have 20 to 60 people and the occasion is drinks rather than a private event, seats on the Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise cost $79 an adult, with 100 seats a sailing and a published schedule. You share the vessel, which for team drinks or a birthday is usually the point rather than a compromise. A charter makes sense when you need the boat to yourselves, the route to be yours, or the guest list to be above about 60.</p><h2>What Boat Time has, so you can size the question</h2><p><strong>Sun Goddess:</strong> 114 feet, up to 135 guests on a day charter, two entertainment decks, two licensed bars.</p><p><strong>Mermaid Spirit:</strong> a 100 foot tri-deck catamaran, up to 150 guests by day, 11 en suite cabins sleeping 22 overnight, chef''s kitchen, stinger pool.</p><p>Both depart Marine Stadium Jetty &amp; Pontoon at Main Beach, roughly ten minutes from Surfers Paradise, with free parking at the pontoon.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How much does it cost to charter a yacht on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>It is quoted rather than listed, because the price moves with the date, the duration, the guest count, the catering and the bar package. A date and a rough guest count are enough to get a real number.</p><h3>Why do you not publish a price?</h3><p>Because a single figure would have to assume the most expensive version of every variable. A charter has no fixed route, duration or inclusion list until you set them, and each of those changes the cost.</p><h3>Are the hourly rates on boat hire websites accurate?</h3><p>They are set by the website publishing them rather than by the operator, and they often include that site''s margin or describe a package the operator does not sell. Check any such figure against a direct quote before budgeting around it.</p><h3>What is the cheapest way to get a group on the water on the Gold Coast?</h3><p>For 20 to 60 people where you do not need the vessel to yourselves, seats on the Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise are $79 an adult, with 100 seats a sailing. A charter is the answer when you need exclusivity, a custom route, or more than about 60 guests.</p><h3>What do you need from me to quote a charter?</h3><p>A date or a few candidate dates, a rough guest count, roughly how long you want to be out, and the kind of catering you have in mind.</p><h2>Ask for the number</h2><p>Send the four things above. The date is the part that runs out, especially on a Saturday and especially in December.</p>', updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-much-does-it-cost-to-charter-a-yacht-on-the-gold-coast'
  and content like '%## %'
  and content not like '%<h2>%';

commit;
